A||||4||William Federle||Oil in Exhaust||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||11-23-2000||07:50 AM||||I've had my 1984 Fiero SE for about 1 1/2 yrs now. It never has had good compression and I always have gotten a little smoke when starting it up, but when it gets up to operating temperature, it must tighten up because I don't have any blue clouds of smoke coming out as I drive down the highway.<P>Now, though, I notice that I have oil droplets on the left rear bumper right above the exhaust. I'm thinking that the engine is getting looser and when it's cold I'm getting more oil by the rings. <P>Has anyone else had this problem? I can't really put another engine in it right now. Is there some product that you can use to reduce this oil loss when the engine is cold?<P>Thanks in advance for any replies.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||11-23-2000||09:57 AM||||Are you sure your "oil droplets" are not just carbon and water droplets from the exhaust ?<P>I would guess in order for oil to show up like this on your rear bumper that you'd almost have to be burning a quart every 100 miles or so. And fouling plugs about as fast.<P>Most all the oil additives are band-aids that really don't solve anything. STP will reduce oil consumption at the expense of increased start-up wear.<P>I would suggest you try some Marvel Mystery Oil in your crankcase and some very potent combustion chamber cleaner in your gas tank (or try the "water" decarbonizing method) as I'm guessing your rings are sticking.<P>I'd also be concerned about the condition of your fuel injector. Try "freezing" the spray pattern by flashing it with your timing light if you have one. Should be a nice conical shape with uniform spray. If the spray pattern looks irregular, with large droplets, it will contribute to carbon buildup in your chambers and a lot of this will come out the tailpipe. If you notice this, try the in-tank cleaner and see if it solves your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||11-23-2000||11:51 AM||||Thanks. I'll try looking at the injector pattern like you suggested and using a decarbonizing agent. <P>I changed the plugs about 3000 miles ago and none of them had black soot or oil on them - they were gray in color.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 11-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||007DOUG||11-23-2000||10:56 PM||||I would guess you have a oil leak under the car. When driving, the excess oil build-up is thrown from under the car onto the bumper.||||reg||1
Z||000004||gixxer||11-23-2000||11:48 PM||||A little puff of oil smoke coming out at startup is usually worn valve stem seals/valve guides. The oil drbbles down the valve stem overnight and drops in the combustion chamber. It then burns when you first start it up in the morn. It can go on like this for a long, long, long time without getting any worse. ||||reg||1
